<p><a href="https://github.com/bbatsov/projectile">Projectile</a> asks you a lot of questions.
What do you want to search for? Replace it with what? Which command should I
run? For as long as I can remember those prompts were about as dumb as a prompt
can be - an empty line, maybe with the symbol at point as the default, and
you had to type the rest.</p>

<p>That’s a bit silly when you think about it, because by the time Projectile asks
you something it already knows quite a lot - the project you’re in, the function
your cursor is sitting in, the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Makefile</code> at the root of the project and what’s
in it. I’ve been meaning to do something about this for ages, and in the
upcoming Projectile 3.5 a bunch of prompts finally start to use that knowledge.
Let’s go over them!</p>

<h2 id="the-search-prompt">The Search Prompt</h2>

<p>Every command that asks what to search for (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-search</code>,
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-grep</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-ripgrep</code>, the search reviewer,
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-find-references</code> and friends) now uses the same prompt. It’s still a
plain text prompt, so whatever you type is what gets searched for, but it has
learned a few new tricks:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">RET</code> on an empty prompt searches for the symbol at point (or the region), as before</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> goes through a few more guesses - the name of the function you’re in,
the name of the current file and the last thing you looked for with Isearch</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-p</code> goes through your previous searches, and the search prompts now have a
history of their own</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">TAB</code> completes the word before point against the identifiers that occur in the project (super handy)</li>
</ul>

<p>Here’s all of this in action:</p>

<p><img src="/assets/images/projectile-search-prompt.gif" alt="The search prompt going through its suggestions with M-n and then completing a typed prefix against the project's identifiers with TAB" /></p>

<p>The <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">TAB</code> completion is my favorite part. I can never remember whether something
was named <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-replace-review</code> or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-review-replace</code>, and now I
don’t have to! The candidates are sorted by how often they show up in the
project, so the names you actually use end up at the top.</p>

<p>Where do the candidates come from? There’s no index and no tags file involved.
Projectile just runs <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">rg</code> (or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">git grep</code>, if you don’t have ripgrep installed)
for the prefix you’ve typed and collects the words that start with it. That
takes a few milliseconds on a small project and a fraction of a second on
something as big as Emacs itself. Not too shabby, right? You need to type at
least two characters for the completion to kick in. And if you don’t like it,
you can always turn it off:</p>

<div class="language-emacs-lisp highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="k">setq</span> <span class="nv">projectile-search-complete-identifiers</span> <span class="no">nil</span><span class="p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><strong>Note:</strong> You might be wondering why the prompt isn’t simply using
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">completing-read</code>. That would have been the easiest way to get completion, but
with something like Vertico pressing <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">RET</code> would then select the current
candidate instead of what you’ve typed, and I really don’t want a search prompt
that second-guesses my input. That’s why it’s a plain <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">read-from-minibuffer</code> with a
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">completion-at-point</code> function installed in it, so <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">TAB</code> works the same way it
does in a code buffer. In the screencast above the candidates are displayed by
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">consult-completion-in-region</code>. Without it you’ll get the stock <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">*Completions*</code>
buffer.</p>

<h2 id="the-replace-prompts">The Replace Prompts</h2>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-replace</code> reads what to replace with the same prompt, so everything
above applies there as well. When it asks what to replace it <em>with</em>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code>
brings up the original text. After all, most replacements are a small edit of
the thing they replace (e.g. a rename), so it’s nice not to have to type it
again.</p>

<p>With a prefix argument the search and replace commands ask which files to work
on, and those prompts now complete over the file extensions that are actually
present in the project. (the most common ones come first)</p>

<h2 id="the-command-prompts">The Command Prompts</h2>

<p>The compile, test and run prompts got the same treatment. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-p</code> has always gone
through the commands you’ve run before. Now <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> goes the other way - through
commands you haven’t run yet, but the project suggests - the default command for
the project’s type, followed by the commands of its
<a href="https://docs.projectile.mx/projectile/tasks.html">tasks</a> (Make targets, npm
scripts, just recipes and so on). The tasks named after what you’re doing come
first, so the test prompt starts with things like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">make test</code>:</p>

<p><img src="/assets/images/projectile-command-prompt.gif" alt="The test command prompt going from npm test through the project's test tasks with M-n" /></p>

<p>The same suggestions show up on <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> when you run a shell command in the
project root with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">s-p !</code> or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">s-p &amp;</code>.</p>

<h2 id="filtering-search-results">Filtering Search Results</h2>

<p>Projectile’s search and replace results buffers have had filters for a while -
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">k</code>/<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">d</code> to keep or flush matches by line, and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">K</code>/<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">D</code> to do the same by file.
The file filters expect a regular expression and most of the time people type
pretty much the same thing there - something like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">^test/</code>.</p>

<p>Now the prompt suggests it for you. The default is the directory of the match at point,
and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> goes on to that file’s extension, the file itself and then the
top-level directories that have matches, busiest first. Getting rid of all the
matches in the tests is <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">D RET</code>:</p>

<p><img src="/assets/images/projectile-filter-prompt.gif" alt="Flushing every match under test/ from the search results by accepting the default the prompt suggests" /></p>

<h2 id="the-find-file-prompt">The Find File Prompt</h2>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-find-file</code> (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">s-p f</code>) is probably the most used Projectile command,
and quite often the file you’re after is one that the current buffer already
points to - the module imported on the current line, a file mentioned in a
backtrace, the test for the code you’re editing. Projectile has had dedicated
commands for all of those forever (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-find-file-dwim</code>,
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-toggle-between-implementation-and-test</code>,
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-find-other-file</code>), but you have to remember them and their
keybindings.</p>

<p>Now <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> at the plain old find file prompt brings up the file named at point,
followed by the test of the current file (or its implementation, if you’re in a
test) and its “other” files. After that it keeps going through the rest of the
project’s files, as before.</p>

<p><img src="/assets/images/projectile-find-file-suggestions.gif" alt="The find file prompt bringing up the file imported at point with M-n and then the test of the current file" /></p>

<p>Projectile is pretty relaxed about what counts as a file name at point - a
bare name, a relative path, an import without the file extension, something
like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">cart.js:42</code> in a compilation buffer - they all work.</p>

<p><strong>Note:</strong> Those are suggestions on <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> and not the default value of the
prompt for a reason. Completion UIs like Vertico move the default to the top of
the candidate list, and that spot belongs to the file you’ve been visiting the
most recently and the most often. Pressing <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">RET</code> right away should keep doing
what it has always done.</p>

<h2 id="odds-and-ends">Odds and Ends</h2>

<ul>
  <li>
    <p>A few prompts that offer a handful of options now have a sensible default.
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-switch-to-buffer</code> defaults to the project buffer you were in
last (just like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">C-x b</code>), <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-run-task</code> to the task you ran last, and
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-find-file-in-subproject</code> to the subproject of the current file.</p>
  </li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">C-u s-p C</code> (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-find-changed-file</code> with a prefix argument) asks for a
revision to compare against. It now defaults to the project’s main branch,
whatever it happens to be called, and it completes over tags as well, so
“what changed since the last release” is a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> or two away.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-add-known-project</code> starts at the root of the project you’re in,
instead of whatever subdirectory the current buffer lives in.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="m-n-is-underrated">M-n is Underrated</h2>

<p>You’ve probably noticed that <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> shows up in pretty much every section of
this article. Every Emacs user knows that <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-p</code> in the minibuffer goes back
through the history. I’m not sure how many people know that <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> goes
<em>forward</em>, into what the Emacs manual calls “future history” - a list of
guesses that the command has made about what you might want to type. A lot of
built-in commands provide those (try <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> at the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">query-replace</code> or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">find-file</code>
prompts), but you’ll never see them unless you ask, so they are easy to miss.
It’s one of those fairly obscure Emacs features that really deserve to be
better known.</p>

<p>For package authors that’s probably the cheapest way to make a prompt smarter.
The functions that read from the minibuffer (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">read-string</code>,
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">read-from-minibuffer</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">completing-read</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">read-shell-command</code>) all accept a
list as their default value, and that list is what <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> goes through:</p>

<div class="language-emacs-lisp highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">read-string</span> <span class="s">"Search for: "</span> <span class="no">nil</span> <span class="ss">'my-search-history</span>
             <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">delq</span> <span class="no">nil</span>
                   <span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">list</span> <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">thing-at-point</span> <span class="ss">'symbol</span> <span class="no">t</span><span class="p">)</span>
                         <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">add-log-current-defun</span><span class="p">)</span>
                         <span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">and</span> <span class="nv">buffer-file-name</span>
                              <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">file-name-base</span> <span class="nv">buffer-file-name</span><span class="p">)))))</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>It doesn’t get in anyone’s way, it works with every completion framework, and
it’s only a few lines of code. I wrote a bit more about <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code>
<a href="/blog/2026/11/27/m-n-and-the-minibuffer-future-history/">here</a>, as it
certainly deserves an article of its own.</p>

<h2 id="closing-thoughts">Closing Thoughts</h2>

<p>None of those changes is a big deal on its own, but together they make
Projectile feel a lot smarter to me. All of this will be a part of Projectile
3.5, and it’s already available in the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">master</code> branch if you want to play with
it today. If you can think of other prompts that could do a better job
of guessing what you want - I’d love to hear about them!</p>

<p>That’s all I have for you today. Keep hacking!</p>